y] \u2014 carry \u00b1$6 and \u00b1$15 per share of valuation uncertainty. Even\nat the top of that combined range, intrinsic value remains below $160.\n```"}, {"sample": 2, "iv": 152.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 509, "report": "```markdown\n# INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS ENGINE v2.0 \u2014 FINAL REPORT \u2014 ITT INC. (ITT)\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 0 \u2014 ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Ticker / Sector / Industry | ITT / Industrials / Specialty Industrial Machinery |\n| T0 Reference | 2026-08-24 [Aggregator quote] |\n| Price T0 | $205.25 [Actual \u2014 vendor quote] |\n| Market Cap | $18.604B [Actual \u2014 vendor] |\n| Shares Outstanding (diluted, est. post-SPX FLOW) | ~83.1M [Estimate \u2014 FY2025 wtd-avg 79.9M + 3.84M shares issued for SPX FLOW on Mar-2-2026 (panabee.com), weighted 10/12 yr] |\n| Enterprise Value (adj. for est. net debt) | ~$21.7B [Estimate] |\n| Base Intrinsic Value (scenario-weighted) | **$152** |\n| Margin of Safety at T0 | **-$53 / -26% (negative)** |\n| Final Recommendation | **HOLD / DO NOT INITIATE \u2014 WAIT FOR LOWER ENTRY** |\n| Conviction | **7/15 (Medium-Low)** |\n\n**Reason for Engine Selection**\n\n- **Archetype: B (Quality Compounder)** \u2014 12-year history of revenue CAGR +3.5% (2014\u201325) with consistent margin expansion (operating margin 10.0% \u2192 17.4%), FCF conversion ~90%, disciplined buybacks (share count -14% over 11 years), and 12-year Piotroski F-score 6/9.\n- **Engine: Engine 1 (DCF + Multiples)** \u2014 the appropriate engine for a quality compounder.\n- **Transition note (v2.0):** The SPX FLOW acquisition ($4.775B, closed 2026-03-02) moves the company toward **Archetype D (Product-Platform Hybrid / scale-cyclical)** \u2014 the Flow segment becomes the largest revenue line and adds chemical/food/pharma/energy cyclicality. We apply **60% Quality Compounder / 40% Cyclical** lens in scenario weights. Transition criteria 50\u201380% met \u2192 **Blended engine, Conviction -0.5 steps**.\n- Alternative engines considered: Engine 2 (Cycle-Adjusted) would lower IV materially; Engine 3 (Hybrid) would raise it slightly. Using Engine 1 with a 40% cyclical overlay is the balanced choice.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT (T0 = August 2026)\n\n**Step 1-1 \u2014 Macro Variables [Actual / Estimate]**\n\n| Variable | Value | Source / Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10Y Treasury Yield | 4.65\u20134.74% | [Actual \u2014 YCharts / FRED 2026-08-21] |\n| Real Rates | ~1.8\u20132.0% (10Y TIPS context) | [Estimate] |\n| Fed Funds Target | 3.50\u20133.75%, on hold 5th consecutive meeting; ~1-in-3 market probability of a hike | [Actual \u2014 Trading Economics / Forbes Advisor Aug-2026] |\n| Fed Stance | Neutral-to-Tightening; pause with hawkish tilt | [Estimate] |\n| DXY | Not held in dataset | [Unconfirmed] |\n| Global Liquidity | Tightening pause; QT still in effect | [Assumption] |\n| Economic Cycle | Late Expansion \u2014 industrial PMI softening, inflation mixed | [Estimate] |\n| Inflation | Elevated vs target; mixed signals | [Estimate] |\n| Credit Spreads | Investment-grade tight; high-yield moderate | [Assumption] |\n\n**Step 1-2 \u2014 Regime Probabilities**\n\n| Regime | Probability |\n|---|---|\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 20% |\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 40% |\n| Stagflation (high rates + low growth) | 25% |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 15% |\n\n**Step 1-3 \u2014 Sector Macro Sensitivity (each -3 to +3)**\n\n| Axis | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest rate sensitivity | -1.5 | Modest rate impact; industrial demand, funding cost |\n| Dollar sensitivity | -1.0 | ~40\u201350% int\u2019l revenue |\n| Liquidity sensitivity | -0.5 | M&A-driven leverage adds sensitivity |\n| Economic cycle sensitivity | -2.0 | Flow/Motion tied to industrial capex and auto production |\n| Commodity sensitivity | -1.0 | Input costs (metals, energy) \u2014 partially hedged |\n\n**Step 1-4 \u2014 Macro Alignment**\n\nWeighted macro impact: **-0.9 (slight headwind)**\n- Regime-weighted: Reflation 40% (neutral-to-positive for industrials), Stagflation 25% (negative), Recession 15% (negative), Goldilocks 20% (positive).\n- **Conviction: -0.5 steps** (already reflected in final score).\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE CHECK\n\n**Step 1.5-1 \u2014 Similar Enterprise Base Rates**\n\n| Question | Base Rate | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Survival of large-cap industrial compounder over 5 years | ~85\u201390% | [Estimate \u2014 Damodaran database] |\n| Large M&A ($1\u201310B) achieving ROIC > WACC in 5 yrs | ~35\u201350% | [Estimate \u2014 McKinsey M&A database] |\n| Company in current financial state (positive FCF, good margins) being profitable in 5 yrs | >90% | [Estimate] |\n| SPX FLOW-type integration delivering announced synergies | ~60\u201370% (cost synergies) | [Estimate \u2014 BCG integration study] |\n| Cyclical industrial peak \u2192 40%+ drawdown within 3 yrs | ~30\u201340% | [Estimate] |\n\n**Step 1.5-2 \u2014 Deviation from Base Rate**\n\nOur base case assumes:\n- Adjusted EPS grows from $8.22 (2026E) to $12.70 (2030E) \u2014 11% CAGR; success probability ~55%.\n- This is slightly above the 35\u201350% M&A base rate. Justifications:\n  1. ITT has a 12-year track record of margin expansion and disciplined capital allocation (capital allocation score 12/15) \u2014 higher-than-average integration capability.\n  2. SPX FLOW cost synergies are modest relative to purchase price \u2014 $80M/$4.775B = 1.7% of price; therefore synergy risk is lower.\n  3. The company raised guidance in Q2 2026 (FY2026 adjusted EPS $8.22 mid-point) with 53% order growth and 13% organic growth \u2014 early evidence of successful launch.\n\n**Step 1.5-3 \u2014 Base Rate Shock Check**\n\n- Success probability (55%) < 1.5\u00d7 base rate (52.5\u201375%) \u2192 no red flag. But it is at the upper edge; we maintain the 25% bear weight.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n**Step 2-1 \u2014 Business Understanding**\n\nITT designs and manufactures engineered critical components: brake pads/friction products for automotive and rail (Motion Technologies), pumps, valves and process equipment for chemical, food/pharma and energy (Industrial Process / Flow Technologies), and connectors, actuation and controls for aerospace/defense and industrial (Connect & Control Technologies). The SPX FLOW acquisition (close 2026-03-02) adds mixers, pumps and sanitary process equipment for biopharm, food, beverage and chemical end-markets.\n\nUnderstandability: **High** \u2014 engineered industrial components with clear end-markets and aftermarket annuity streams.\n\n**Step 2-2 \u2014 Moat Score (7 types, 0-3 each)**\n\n| Moat Type | Score | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brands: Goulds, Kollmorgen, Cannon; specs) | 2 | Stable |\n| Switching costs (certified components, aftermarket part numbers) | 3 | Stable |\n| Network effects | 0 | n/a |\n| Cost advantage (scale/pricing) | 1 | Narrowing vs larger Chinese/auto suppliers |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening (SPX FLOW scale) |\n| Regulatory barriers (defense approvals, SAE/TUV certs) | 2 | Stable |\n| Data moat (condition monitoring, digital service) | 1 | Widening |\n| **Total** | **11/21** | **Narrow-to-Wide** |\n\n**Moat direction:** Stable; modest widening in CCT via aerospace and digital services.\n\n**Step 2-3 \u2014 Lynch Classification**\n\n**Fast Grower on the edge of Stalwart** \u2014 FY2014-25 revenue CAGR 3.5% (reported), organic growth 3\u20135% pre-acquisition; FY2026E organic ~5-6%, EPS growth 15\u201318% from SPX FLOW and synergies.\n\n**Step 2-4 \u2014 Financial Strength Scorecard**\n\n| Metric | FY2023 | FY2024 | FY2025 | TTM | Tag |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue growth (3-yr CAGR) | \u2014 | \u2014 | +9.7% (5-yr) | +51.5% Q2'26 | [Actual] |\n| Operating margin | 16.1% | 18.7% | 17.4% | 17.4% | [Actual] |\n| Net margin | 12.5% | 14.3% | 12.4% | 8.9% | [Actual/Estimate] |\n| ROE (GAAP) | ~16.2% | ~18.7% | ~11.9% | ~10.1% | [Arithmetic] |\n| ROIC (reported) | ~19% | ~24% | ~15% | n/a | [Estimate] |\n| FCF Conversion (FCF/NI) | 105% | 84% | 112% | n/a | [Actual] |\n| Net Debt / EBITDA (pre-deal) | net cash | net cash | net cash | ~2.2\u00d7 post-SPX | [Estimate] |\n| Interest Coverage (pre-deal) | >50\u00d7 | >50\u00d7 | ~40\u00d7 | ~7.5\u00d7 post-deal | [Estimate] |\n\n**Judgment: Good** \u2014 pre-deal balance sheet pristine; post-deal leverage ~2.2\u00d7 EBITDA is manageable but a step-change.\n\n**Step 2-5 \u2014 Capital Allocation Scorecard (from pack + research)**\n\n1. Buyback efficiency: **2/3** \u2014 consistent buybacks; shares -14% from 2014 to 2025.\n2. M&A track record: **2/3** \u2014 SPX FLOW $4.775B at ~8.5\u00d7 EBITDA [Estimate]; synergies $80M (1.7% of price) \u2014 disciplined but contingent on execution.\n3. Capex efficiency: **3/3** \u2014 capex intensity ~3%; incremental ROIC >20% historically.\n4. Rationality of mix: **2/3** \u2014 funding 43% equity / 57% debt; preserves flexibility.\n5. Guidance accuracy: **3/3** \u2014 Q2 2026 beat; raised full-year guidance (adjusted EPS $8.22 midpoint vs $7.9 prior).\n\nInsider ownership: 0.52% [Actual \u2014 Aggregator]; no recent insider buy/sell data \u2192 no bonus.\n\n**Total: 12/15 \u2014 Good**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS (v2.0)\n\n**Step 2.5-1 \u2014 R&D Capitalization**\n\nITT R&D (within SG&A) not separately broken out in pack \u2014 estimated $60\u201380M annually in SG&A of $0.368B FY2025 [Assumption]. Capitalizing and amortizing 4 years adds ~$50M to adjusted operating income, ~$250M to invested capital. Adjusted ROIC impact: ~+2pct.\n\n**Step 2.5-2 \u2014 SBC Treatment**\n\n- FY2025 SBC: $0.036B [Actual]\n- TTM SBC: ~$0.040B [Estimate]\n- Owner FCF adjusted: FY2025 FCF $0.547B \u2212 SBC $0.036B = **$0.511B** [Actual arithmetic \u2014 per pack instruction]\n\n**Step 2.5-3 \u2014 Operating Lease Capitalization**\n\nFY2025 operating leases $0.084B [Actual] \u2014 capitalize at 8\u00d7 \u2192 +$0.67B debt; adjusted net debt rises ~$0.6B; coverage remains >5\u00d7.\n\n**Step 2.5-4 \u2014 Brand / Data Assets**\n\nBrand values (Goulds, Kollmorgen, Cannon, SPX FLOW brands) rough estimate $1\u20132B [Assumption]. Data assets (IoT condition monitoring) early-stage \u2014 minimal.\n\n**Step 2.5-5 \u2014 Adjusted ROIC**\n\nAdjusted NOPAT (FY2025): ~$0.80B [Estimate \u2014 adding back R&D cap, SBC included as expense]\nAdjusted Invested Capital: ~$3.5B\n**Adjusted ROIC: ~23%** versus GAAP ROIC ~15% [Estimate]\nGap >5pct due largely to R&D, brand, and lease capitalization.\n\n**Step 2.5-6 \u2014 Owner Earnings (Buffett)**\n\n- FY2025 Owner Earnings = NI $0.488B + D&A $0.143B \u2212 Maintenance Capex ($0.08B est.) \u2212 \u0394WC ($0.002B) = **~$0.549B** [Estimate]\n- FY2026E Owner Earnings = Adjusted NI $0.670B + D&A ex-acquisition amortization $0.160B \u2212 Maint. capex $0.090B \u2212 \u0394WC $0.010B = **~$0.730B** [Estimate/Assumption]\n- Deduct SBC: \u2192 ~$0.690B [Estimate]\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\n**Step 3-1 \u2014 WACC Derivation**\n\n| Component | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate (10Y) | 4.65% | [Actual \u2014 YCharts 2026-08-21] |\n| Beta | 1.28 | [Aggregator \u2014 pack, vendor window unstated] |\n| Equity risk premium | 4.4% | [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran long-run U.S.] |\n| Cost of equity | 4.65 + 1.28 \u00d7 4.4 = **10.3%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt (new term loan) | 5.5% | [Assumption \u2014 SOFR ~4.3% + 150\u2013200bp; S&P article confirms $2.8B 2-yr term loan] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 5.5% \u00d7 (1\u22120.24) = 4.2% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Weights: E=86%, D=14% | Using market cap $18.604B and net debt $3.07B | [Estimate] |\n| **WACC** | **9.5%** | [Arithmetic] |\n\n**Step 3-2 \u2014 Explicit Forecast (all figures USD millions)**\n\n| FY | Revenue | GAAP EBIT | EBIT% | NOPAT | +D&A | \u2212Capex | \u2212\u0394WC | FCFF | Key Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026E | 5,650 | 780 | 13.8% | 593 | 400 | 175 | 10 | 808 | H1 actual $2.685B; H2E $2.96B; D&A incl. ~$250M purchase amortization [Estimate/Assumption] |\n| 2027E | 6,000 | 860 | 14.3% | 654 | 390 | 185 | 15 | 844 | +5% organic; SPX full-year; synergy $30M [Assumption] |\n| 2028E | 6,300 | 930 | 14.8% | 707 | 380 | 195 | 15 | 877 | +5% organic; synergy $55M; D&A declines [Assumption] |\n| 2029E | 6,600 | 995 | 15.1% | 756 | 370 | 205 | 15 | 906 | +4.8% organic [Assumption] |\n| 2030E | 6,900 | 1,060 | 15.4% | 806 | 360 | 215 | 20 | 931 | +4.5% organic; automation/capex slight increase [Assumption] |\n\n**Terminal value** = $931 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.095 \u2212 0.03) = **$14,756M** [Assumption: terminal growth 3.0%, long-run nominal GDP]\n\n**DCF Value:**\n\n| Item | PV (US$M) |\n|---|---|\n| FY2026 FCFF | 772 |\n| FY2027 FCFF | 736 |\n| FY2028 FCFF | 731 |\n| FY2029 FCFF | 722 |\n| FY2030 FCFF | 709 |\n| PV of Terminal Value | 11,237 |\n| **Enterprise Value** | **14,907** |\n| Less: Net debt (est.) | (3,066) |\n| Plus: Cash & ST investments | included in net-debt calc |\n| **Equity Value** | **11,841** |\n| Diluted shares (est.) | 83.1M |\n| **DCF value per share** | **$142.5** |\n\n**Revenue growth, margin, capex, and discount-rate sources:**\n- Revenue FY2026: H1 actual ($2.685B) + H2 estimate from company guidance raise (\"raising full year guidance on revenue, margin, EPS and cash\" \u2014 Q2 2026 release, investors.itt.com). H2 revenue growth ~30\u201335% implied by H1 + guides.\n- Revenue FY2027\u20132030: **No guided figures.** Company only guides organic growth 5% for FY2026 (investing.com). We assume 4.5\u20135% organic for 2027\u20132030 [Assumption]. If organic growth is 7%, IV rises to ~$165; if 3%, IV falls to ~$130. Range shown rather than a single point.\n- EBIT margin: FY2025 GAAP op margin 17.4% [Actual]. We model GAAP margin 13.8\u201315.4% because purchase amortization (~$250M/yr \u2014 [Estimate based on SPX FLOW $4.775B purchase, ~$2.5B intangibles, 10-yr amortization]) depresses GAAP EBIT. Adjusted EBIT margin is ~17.2% by 2026E and ~18.4% by 2030E [Assumption \u2014 synergy realization].\n- Capex: 3.1% of revenue, consistent with FY2024\u201325 actuals (3.4% / 3.1%) and SPX FLOW asset-light profile [Assumption].\n- D&A: Legacy 3.6% of revenue (~$143M FY2025 [Actual]) + SPX FLOW PP&E D&A ~$80M [Estimate] + purchase amortization ~$250M [Estimate] \u2192 normalized to $360\u2013400M [Assumption].\n- WACC: see table above. If WACC is 8.5% (market-implied low beta), IV rises to ~$175; if 10.5%, IV falls to ~$125.\n\n**Step 3-3 \u2014 Reverse DCF (Market-Implied Expectations)**\n\nAt $205.25, market cap $18.604B, EV ~$21.7B. Solving for constant FCF growth from base FCFF $808M:\n$21.7B = $808M \u00d7 (1+g)/(0.095\u2212g) \u2192 **g \u2248 5.6%**\n\nThat is **1.9\u00d7 the long-run nominal GDP assumption** used in our terminal value. The market is pricing perpetual 5.6% FCFF growth \u2014 feasible only if SPX FLOW not only delivers full synergies but also accelerates organic growth to 7%+ for a decade with stable 18%+ adjusted margins. **Excessive, in our view.**\n\n**Step 3-4 \u2014 Relative Multiples**\n\n| Multiple | ITT | Peers (DOV, ROK, EMR, PH) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Forward P/E (2026 adjusted) | 25.0\u00d7 | 18\u201322\u00d7 [Estimate] |\n| EV/EBITDA (2026E adj.) | ~16.5\u00d7 | 12\u201315\u00d7 [Estimate] |\n| P/B | 4.09\u00d7 | 3\u20135\u00d7 [Estimate] |\n| 5-yr PEG (adj. EPS growth) | 25.0 / 16.4 = 1.52 | 1.2\u20131.6 [Estimate] |\n\n**Conclusion:** Valuation is at the top of the quality-industrial range; no margin of safety on comparable basis.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n**Step 3.5-1 \u2014 Competitive Response**\n\nIf ITT executes SPX FLOW integration well and reaches 14\u201315% GAAP EBIT margins, competitors (Dover, Alfa Laval, Xylem) will defend share \u2014 price competition in flow is plausible, shaving ~0.5\u20131.0pt of margin by 2028. We reflect this in our conservative 15.4% GAAP EBIT margin ceiling. **[Assumption]**\n\n**Step 3.5-2 \u2014 Reflexivity**\n\n- Positive: Stock at $205 enables SBC attraction and cheap equity currency for bolt-ons (already deployed \u2014 Aerospace Contacts for $31M closed July 2026).\n- Negative: If integration stumbles, de-rating to 18\u00d7 would amplify via talent attrition and financing difficulties \u2014 no immediate reflexivity risk given BBB rating.\n\n**Step 3.5-3 \u2014 Customer Behavior Evolution**\n\n- SPX FLOW's food/pharma biotech end-markets are early-adopter to mainstream phases \u2014 demand durability high; as penetration grows, LTV 5\u20138\u00d7, CAC rising \u2014 net retention ~105%? [Unconfirmed \u2014 no churn data held]\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n**Step 4-1/2 \u2014 Scenario Composition**\n\n| Scenario | Prob. | Description | 2027E Adj. EPS | 2028E EPS | Terminal P/E | 1-Yr Price |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bear | 25% | Integration fails, organic 2%, recession, synergies missed, leverage binds; GAAP vs adj. gap under-scrutiny | $8.90 | $9.60 | 13\u00d7 | $123 |\n| Base | 50% | Integration OK, organic 4\u20135%, synergies on schedule; WACC 9.5% | $9.57 | $10.60 | 17\u00d7 | $163 |\n| Bull | 25% | Integration excellent, aerospace supercycle, organic 7%, margins beat; market pays 21\u00d7 | $10.25 | $11.85 | 21\u00d7 | $215 |\n\n**Step 4-3 \u2014 Bayesian Update**\n\n- Prior (from Layer 1.5): success prob. 55%.\n- Evidence: Q2 2026 adjusted EPS $2.08 vs. consensus $1.93 (beat $0.15); organic revenue +13%; orders +53%; guidance raised \u2014 likelihood ratio ~1.4\u00d7.\n- Posterior success prob: ~65%. We offset with macro headwind (-5pct) and interim CFO flag (-5pct) \u2192 **55% base-case weight remains appropriate.**\n\n**Step 4-4/5 \u2014 Expected Price & Margin of Safety**\n\n- Expected 1-yr price = 0.25\u00d7123 + 0.50\u00d7163 + 0.25\u00d7215 = *